<?xml version='1.0' standalone='yes'?>
<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
<!ENTITY name      "restic.backup">
<!ENTITY author    "MajorPain007">
<!ENTITY version   "2026.10.05.01">
<!ENTITY md5       "26de3388134271834b84d84c90519b72">
<!ENTITY launch    "Settings/ResticBackup">
<!ENTITY github    "MajorPain007/unraid-restic-backup">
<!ENTITY pluginURL "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/&github;/main/src/&name;.plg">
<!ENTITY pkgURL    "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/&github;/main/archive">
<!ENTITY pluginDir "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;">
<!ENTITY configDir "/boot/config/plugins/&name;">
<!ENTITY resticDir "/usr/local/lib/&name;">
<!ENTITY resticVer "0.19.1">
<!ENTITY resticSha "f415415624dcc452f2a02b8c33641791a8c6d6d3b65bbb3543fcf9a25151585c">
]>

<PLUGIN name="&name;" author="&author;" version="&version;" launch="&launch;"
        pluginURL="&pluginURL;"
        min="6.12.0"
        icon="history">

<CHANGES>
## Version 2026.10.05.01 - Data folder on a snapshotted pool

- A job backing up from a ZFS snapshot failed at once with read-only file
  system when the plugin's data folder was on the same dataset, such as
  /mnt/cache/appdata/restic.backup for a job backing up /mnt/cache/appdata:
  restic saw its cache folder in the snapshot and could not write to it.
  The cache and temporary folders now stay the live, writable ones.

## Version 2026.10.04.05 - No false alarm after installing

- Right after installing, the page said the scheduler had not run yet,
  until cron started it at the next full minute. It now waits three
  minutes before it says so.

## Version 2026.10.04.04 - Leaner source

- The comments are gone from the source files. Nothing changes in how the
  plugin works.

## Version 2026.10.04.03 - What gets backed up, at once

- The job editor shows the job's folders as a tree under Leave out, each
  entry marked: a check for backed up, a cross for left out, with the rule
  that leaves it out. A folder is checked against the rules when it opens,
  in milliseconds, as nothing below it is read; the tree follows the rules
  as they are typed.
- A click on the mark leaves a file or folder out or takes it back, and the
  rules follow. What a pattern such as *.log leaves out can be backed up
  anyway.
- What gets backed up, in a job's menu, shows the same tree. Adding up the
  sizes goes through every file and takes minutes for large shares; it is
  now Measure sizes.

## Version 2026.10.04.02 - Browsing snapshots without the wait

- Folders of a snapshot open at once. Until now every folder ran restic
  anew, and restic loads the repository's whole index before it lists
  anything: about 8 seconds per folder for an 850 GB repository, however
  small the folder. Now the first folder starts restic mount, which loads
  the index once and then answers from memory, for every snapshot of the
  repository - a few milliseconds per folder.
- The mount ends after 10 minutes without use, when a backup, a clean-up
  or a check on the repository starts or ends, and when the array stops.
  While it runs it needs memory, about 1 GB for 850 GB of backups. Where it
  cannot work, folders are listed the old way.

## Version 2026.10.04.01 - The plugin's cache stayed in backups

- A job backing up the pool path of appdata, such as /mnt/cache/appdata,
  backed up the plugin's own restic cache, several gigabytes of it: the
  data folder is set as /mnt/user/appdata/restic.backup, and the plugin
  only left it out when a source was written the same way. Both ways are
  now recognised, for the cache, the temporary files and a local
  repository inside a source. The preview shows it under Left out.
- The log of a run lists its cleanup steps plainly instead of as Undoing.

## Version 2026.10.03.09 - Preview and size history

- What gets backed up: a preview of a job, in its menu and in the editor,
  for unsaved changes too. It shows how much a backup would read, what each
  exclude rule leaves out - and which rules match nothing, perhaps misspelt -
  the largest folders and the largest files. It goes through the folders
  with restic's own rules, reading names and sizes only.
- Size history, in the menus of repositories and jobs: a repository's size
  on disk over time, and per job how much each backup read and added.
- Exclude patterns with a dollar sign did not work: restic expands
  environment variables in exclude files, so the recycle bin pattern ending
  in /$RECYCLE.BIN matched folders named .BIN instead. Patterns are now
  passed on literally.

## Version 2026.10.03.08 - Settings icon

- The tile under Settings, Utilities showed Unraid's default icon. It has the
  plugin's own icon now, as the Plugins page does.

## Version 2026.10.03.07 - ZFS where it can, the scheduler after updates

- A job with a ZFS consistency setting backs up what is on ZFS from a
  snapshot and reads the rest - /boot, a share on an array disk - as it is.
  Before, the whole job was refused. The job editor lists the folders read
  as they are.
- After an update the scheduler could be missing from Unraid's crontab, and
  no scheduled backup started. The plugin manager replaces the plugin's
  registration right after the install script, and the script registered
  the schedule in that moment. It now waits for the new registration and
  checks the crontab.
- For a new job, choosing a consistency setting showed nothing below the
  cards: the list of containers to stop was missing.
- The Plugins page shows Restic Backup with a description, and a fitting
  icon.

## Version 2026.10.03.06 - SSH servers

- The command given for putting the key onto a Hetzner Storage Box failed on
  Unraid: ssh-copy-id -s keeps a socket in ~/.ssh, which on Unraid is on the
  flash drive, and FAT allows no sockets. It is Hetzner's own install-ssh-key
  now, which also keeps the keys already there.
- The server choice for SFTP repositories is down to an SSH server - Unraid,
  NAS, Linux, VPS - or a Hetzner Storage Box. All are reached the same way;
  a Storage Box differs only in its port and in how a key gets onto it. A
  Storage Box host name selects it by itself, with port 23.

## Version 2026.10.03.05 - Repository icon

- The icon on a repository card sits in a square tile of its own, centred and
  a little larger. It borrowed the style of the small labels before and could
  come out taller than wide.

## Version 2026.10.03.04 - Folders and colours

- Dialogs lay below Unraid's footer, which covered the buttons at their foot.
  They are above it now.
- The folder picker can make a new folder - for a repository, a restore or
  the data folder - owned and permitted like the folder it is made in. Not in
  /mnt itself, in memory or on the flash drive.
- Folders to back up can be ticked several at a time, from different places
  too.
- What is selected - a snapshot, the bar for ticked files, a chosen option -
  is marked in a cool slate. The accent orange, laid thinly over the dark
  themes, looked brown.
- Check boxes and scroll bars are drawn dark on dark themes instead of white.

## Version 2026.10.03.03 - ZFS snapshots nothing can hold on to

- A container started while a backup ran - one that watchtower updated, say -
  could keep the backup's ZFS snapshot busy, and the snapshot stayed behind.
  The snapshots were opened through .zfs/snapshot, which mounts them for the
  whole server; Docker binds a folder with everything mounted inside it, and
  Unraid starts containers with a copy of all mounts. They are now mounted
  only where restic runs, and no other process sees them.
- Should a container hold a copy anyway - someone opened the snapshot during
  the backup - the copy is unmounted in the container and the snapshot
  removed. A snapshot busy for another reason is tried again every minute
  instead of being left behind.
- Containers and VMs stopped for the whole backup start again as soon as
  restic has read the data, not only after old snapshots are cleaned up and
  the repository is measured. ZFS snapshots are removed at that point too.
- The log line with a backup's result showed no numbers.

## Version 2026.10.03.02 - Fixes from the first run on a real server

- Option descriptions and table text ran over their edges. Unraid's own
  stylesheet keeps every button and table cell on one line; the plugin's styles
  now undo that inside the page. A status class that Unraid colours red is
  renamed.
- Containers that mount a parent folder of a backup - file managers with all of
  /mnt or /mnt/user mounted - counted as users of the data. They were suggested
  for stopping, and every backup with a stop list warned about them. They are
  listed apart now and left running unless chosen.
- Notifications are sent without waiting for them. Unraid runs notification
  agents one after another, and one that did not answer held the scheduler for
  minutes; cron starts no new scheduler while the last one runs, so a scheduled
  backup could be held up meanwhile.
- After an interrupted backup, what cannot be put back at once - a ZFS snapshot
  still busy - is tried again every minute instead of being given up.
- Containers are stopped with the timeout set in Settings, Docker, as Unraid
  does when the array stops.
- ZFS snapshots on more than one pool are taken pool by pool; one zfs snapshot
  command cannot span pools.
- A folder in /mnt that is neither a pool nor a disk, or an empty share folder
  on another disk, no longer makes a share look spread over several.
- The page warns when the scheduler has not run for three minutes.

## Version 2026.10.03.01 - First release

Backups with restic, set up and watched from the Unraid web interface.

- Repositories on a local disk or pool, on an SFTP server - another Unraid, a
  NAS, a Hetzner Storage Box, any Linux server - or on a restic REST server.
  SFTP uses an SSH key the plugin makes for each repository; the server's host
  key is shown and confirmed before the first connection.
- Backup jobs with their own folders, exclusions, schedule and retention.
  Schedules are evaluated by the plugin, not written into the crontab, so a
  run missed while the server was off happens when it is back.
- Data in use: per job, back up as it is, from a ZFS snapshot, from a ZFS
  snapshot taken with the containers and VMs using the data briefly stopped,
  or with them stopped for the whole backup.
- Snapshots: browse, restore files and folders in place or into another
  folder, download, search across snapshots, compare two.
- Prune and check on their own schedules, Unraid notifications, an optional
  Healthchecks ping, commands before and after a backup.
- restic 0.19.1 is downloaded from its GitHub release and checked against the
  SHA-256 published with it.
</CHANGES>

<FILE Name="&configDir;/restic_&resticVer;_linux_amd64.bz2">
<URL>https://github.com/restic/restic/releases/download/v&resticVer;/restic_&resticVer;_linux_amd64.bz2</URL>
<SHA256>&resticSha;</SHA256>
</FILE>

<FILE Name="&configDir;/&name;-&version;-x86_64-1.txz" Run="upgradepkg --install-new">
<URL>&pkgURL;/&name;-&version;-x86_64-1.txz</URL>
<MD5>&md5;</MD5>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
set -u
mkdir -p &resticDir;
if ! bunzip2 -c &configDir;/restic_&resticVer;_linux_amd64.bz2 &gt; &resticDir;/restic.new; then
  echo "Could not unpack restic." &gt;&amp;2
  exit 1
fi
chmod 755 &resticDir;/restic.new
mv -f &resticDir;/restic.new &resticDir;/restic

for f in &configDir;/restic_*_linux_amd64.bz2 &configDir;/&name;-*.txz; do
  case "$f" in
    *_&resticVer;_*|*-&version;-*) ;;
    *) rm -f "$f" ;;
  esac
done

[ -e /usr/local/bin/restic ] || ln -s &resticDir;/restic /usr/local/bin/restic

for d in secrets ssh; do
  mkdir -p &configDir;/$d
  [ -f &configDir;/$d/.gitignore ] || echo '*' &gt; &configDir;/$d/.gitignore
done

CRON='* * * * * /usr/bin/php &pluginDir;/scripts/scheduler.php &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1'
[ "$(cat &configDir;/&name;.cron 2&gt;/dev/null)" = "$CRON" ] || echo "$CRON" &gt; &configDir;/&name;.cron
START=$(date +%s)
( for i in $(seq 1 90); do
    sleep 2
    L=/var/log/plugins/&name;.plg
    { [ -L "$L" ] || [ -e "$L" ]; } &amp;&amp; [ "$(stat -c %Y "$L")" -ge "$START" ] || continue
    crontab -c /etc/cron.d -l 2&gt;/dev/null | grep -qF '&pluginDir;/scripts/scheduler.php' &amp;&amp; break
    /usr/local/sbin/update_cron
  done ) &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1 &lt;/dev/null &amp;

mkdir -p /tmp/&name;
echo
echo "Restic Backup is installed: Settings, Utilities, Restic Backup."
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>
[ -f &pluginDir;/scripts/stop_all.php ] &amp;&amp; /usr/bin/php &pluginDir;/scripts/stop_all.php 60
rm -f &configDir;/&name;.cron
command -v update_cron &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1 &amp;&amp; update_cron &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1
if [ "$(readlink /usr/local/bin/restic 2&gt;/dev/null)" = "&resticDir;/restic" ]; then
  rm -f /usr/local/bin/restic
fi
removepkg &name; 2&gt;/dev/null || rm -rf &pluginDir;
rm -rf &resticDir; /tmp/&name;
echo "Restic Backup removed. Your backups are untouched. Repository passwords, SSH keys and"
echo "settings stay in &configDir; - keep them while you may need the backups."
</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
